# Drizel — Step 8: Giving the Grain Cloud an Interface

Replaced the generic test panel with a dedicated instrument interface.

## What Changed

- Controls for position, pitch, density, output gain, and LFO settings.
- Play toggle and WAV drag-and-drop.
- Sample waveform with a base-position marker.
- Active-grain count and output-level meter.

## How It Works

The processor publishes output peak and active-grain count through
lock-free atomics. The interface reads these independently without
making the audio callback wait.

Sample loading runs on a background thread. A file path and revision
tell the interface when to refresh the waveform, including when the
same file is reloaded. A separate lock protects this pair outside
the audio callback.

The existing LFO processing remains unchanged. This step makes the
engine easier to control and observe.

